== Q2 | Supply and Producer Surplus (B2.1, B2.2)

Use a graph to plot this supply curve. Find and label the producer surplus at a price of 30. On the same graph, show what happens to producer surplus when the price increases to 40.

#grid(
  columns: (2fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 1em,
  [
    a) Quantity supplied at 20 *100*, at 30 *150*, at 40 *200* (B2.1)

    b) What is the marginal cost of the 100th vial? *20* (B2.1)

    c) PS at 30 *2250* PS at 40 *4000* Revenue at 30 *4500* (B2.2)
  ],
  [
    #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
  ],
)

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Supply: Q/5, through the origin, so the first vial costs almost nothing to harvest.

  a) 20 = Q/5 gives Qs = *100*; at 30, *150*; at 40, *200*.

  b) MC = 100/5 = *20*: the height of the supply curve at the 100th vial.

  c) PS at 30 = ½ · 150 · 30 = *2250*, the triangle above supply and below the price. At 40, Qs = 200 and PS = ½ · 200 · 40 = *4000*: up by 1750. Revenue at 30 = 30 · 150 = *4500*, the rectangle under the price; producer surplus is the part of it above the supply curve.

]

