== Q3 | Price Elasticity (B5.2)

a) Use the midpoint method to find the elasticity of demand when the price changes from 10 to 20. *−1/3*

b) Use the midpoint method to find the elasticity of supply when the price changes from 20 to 40. *1*

c) Which of a)–b) is elastic, unit elastic, or inelastic? *a) inelastic, b) unit elastic*

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  a) Q goes from 500 to 400. Percent change in quantity over percent change in price, each measured from the midpoint: (−100/450) ÷ (10/15) = (−2/9) ÷ (2/3) = *−1/3*. Below one in absolute value: *inelastic*.

  b) Q goes from 100 to 200. (100/150) ÷ (20/30) = (2/3) ÷ (2/3) = *1*: *unit elastic*. A supply curve through the origin is unit elastic everywhere, since price and quantity always change in the same proportion.

  c) a) *inelastic*, b) *unit elastic*.

]

