== Q5 | Price Floor (B4.2)

Use a graph of the demand curve and the supply curve to evaluate the welfare effects of a price floor of 45.

#grid(
  columns: (2fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 1em,
  [
    a) CS *1125* Change in CS from Equilibrium *−875*

    b) PS *4500* Change in PS from Equilibrium *+500*

    c) DWL *375* Shortage/Excess *excess of 75*
  ],
  [
    #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
  ],
)

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  The floor of 45 is above P\* = 40, so it binds. Qd: 45 = 60 − Q/10 gives 150. Qs: 45 = Q/5 gives 225. Only 150 vials are exchanged, the smaller of the two, leaving an *excess* of *75*.

  a) CS = ½ · 150 · (60 − 45) = *1125*, down *875* from 2000: buyers pay more and get fewer vials.

  b) PS runs from the price down to supply over the 150 vials traded, where MC = 30: ½ · (45 + 15) · 150 = *4500*, up *500* from 4000. The sellers who still trade gain more than the sellers who are shut out lose.

  c) DWL = ½ · (45 − 30) · (200 − 150) = *375*: the surplus on the 50 trades that no longer happen. Check: 6000 − 1125 − 4500 = 375.

]

