== Q6 | Second Wizarding War (B5.1, B5.3)

The second wizarding war limited the population of dragons. Use a graph to aid your discussion of the impact the war had on the market for dragontear.

#grid(
  columns: (2fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 1em,
  [
    a) The demand curve: shifted up, *stayed the same*, shifted down (B5.1)

    b) The supply curve: *shifted up*, stayed the same, shifted down (B5.1)

    In the years after the war, many more in the wizarding world required dragontear as part of a widespread rise in the popularity of spells. On its own (not including the change in the population of dragons), how has this change in popularity of spells changed the market for dragontear?

    e) The demand curve: *shifted up*, stayed the same, shifted down (B5.1)

    f) The supply curve: shifted up, *stayed the same*, shifted down (B5.1)
  ],
  [
    #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
  ],
)

Without using numbers, how have these two changes together impacted the dragontear market equilibrium?

g) Equilibrium price has: *increased*, stayed the same, decreased, indeterminate (B5.3)

h) Equilibrium quantity has: increased, stayed the same, decreased, *indeterminate* (B5.3)

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  a) Demand *stayed the same*: the war changed the dragons, not what buyers are willing to pay.

  b) Supply *shifted up*: with fewer dragons, each vial costs more to harvest, so sellers need a higher price at every quantity. On its own this raises the price and lowers the quantity.

  e) Demand *shifted up*: more spells need dragontear, so buyers are willing to pay more at every quantity. f) Supply *stayed the same*.

  g) Both shifts push the price the same way, so the price *increased*.

  h) The shifts push quantity opposite ways: supply up lowers it, demand up raises it. Without the sizes of the shifts the change in quantity is *indeterminate*. The graph shows one case, where the supply shift is the larger of the two and quantity falls.

]

